<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Editorial Policy &amp; Corrections &ndash; Edinburgh Festivals Top-Rated Shows</title>
<meta name="description" content="What this leaderboard counts as a review, how ratings are converted, what is shown but not counted, and how to have something corrected.">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ site_url }}/policy.html">
<style>
  :root {
    --ink: #1a1a1a; --muted: #6b6b6b; --rule: #ecdcd9;
    --bg: #f9e4e0; --card: #ffffff;
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0; padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink);
    font: 16px/1.65 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  }
  main { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.7rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 .3rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 2rem 0 .5rem; }
  p, li { margin: .55rem 0; }
  ul { padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  a { color: inherit; }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .box { background: var(--card); border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  .back { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: .95rem; }
  footer { margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
           color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <a class="back" href="./">&larr; Back to the leaderboard</a>
  <h1>Editorial Policy &amp; Corrections</h1>
  <p class="lede">What counts as a review here, what does not, and how to have
  something put right.</p>

  <h2>What is counted</h2>
  <p>A star rating a publication published itself, for a show at the festival in
  the year listed. Every rating links back to the review it came from, so you can
  check it. Nothing is written by us and nothing is inferred: if a publication did
  not award stars, there is no rating to record.</p>

  <h2>Ratings on other scales</h2>
  <p>Converted to five stars, labelled as converted, and rounded <em>down</em>.
  A publication giving 9/10 has said &ldquo;very good, not perfect&rdquo;, and
  rounding that up would put a show on the top line on the strength of a rating
  nobody gave it. The original is always shown beside the conversion.</p>

  <h2>One review, once</h2>
  <p>A publication counts once per show. Where a review carries a byline, two
  different critics at one publication are two reviews &mdash; that is a real
  second opinion. The same review republished at a new address, or reposted with
  the headline slightly changed, is one review however many times it appears.</p>

  <h2>What is not counted</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Awards, longlists and showcase selections.</strong> Real
    recognition, but not a star rating.</li>
    <li><strong>Mentions in a round-up that carry no rating of their own.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Posts on platforms that refuse to be read.</strong> Instagram
    disallows all crawling, so a rating there cannot be verified by us or by
    anyone else, and we do not take it on trust.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews of another year&rsquo;s run.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  {% if greylist %}
  <h2>Shown but not counted</h2>
  <p>Some publications appear on a show&rsquo;s page, credited and linked, without
  affecting its position: {{ greylist|join(", ") }}.</p>
  <p>This is about how much we know, not about the writing. Where we hold only one
  or two ratings from a publication and all of them are five stars, that rating
  cannot tell one show from another &mdash; it has only ever pushed in one
  direction. A publication that goes on to review widely, using the range, is
  counted again. The list is reviewed each year.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <h2>How the order is decided</h2>
  <p>Five stars are worth more than four, and low ratings cost: a show cannot
  climb on volume alone. Shows with no four- or five-star review are listed but
  not ranked. The average shown is the plain average of every counted rating.</p>

  <h2>Tickets</h2>
  <p>Availability comes from the festival&rsquo;s own listings and is refreshed
  daily. &ldquo;No allocation&rdquo; means the festival box office has none left;
  the venue may still sell you one, which is why that is a choice you make in the
  filter rather than an assumption we make for you.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <div class="box">
    <p>Something wrong, missing, or a rating you would like amended or removed?
    Write to <a href="mailto:{{ contact_email }}">{{ contact_email }}</a> and it
    will be actioned.</p>
    <p>Publications: a request to change or remove your own rating is always
    honoured, no reasons needed.</p>
    <p>Companies: please allow 24 hours before writing &mdash; most of what
    arrives is a review we would have collected anyway, a day later. Links help,
    and a rating is only added once we have seen it on the publication&rsquo;s own
    page.</p>
  </div>
</main>
<footer>
  Ratings are collated automatically from publicly available reviews and link back
  to the publication. Star ratings are the publications&rsquo; own; this site only
  counts them.
</footer>
</body>
</html>
